{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00964 - MONDO_5338",
  "verbalization": "Apraclonidine, typically used for its antihypertensive effects, is linked to Open-angle Glaucoma through off-label use and its association with ocular hypertension. The evidence suggests that Apraclonidine, when used outside its approved indications, can contribute to ocular hypertension, which in turn may lead to Open-angle Glaucoma. This relationship is supported by the shared indications of ocular hypertension with multiple drugs, including Befunolol and Dorzolamide, which are also used for glaucoma management. The mechanism appears to involve the elevation of intraocular pressure, a known risk factor for Open-angle Glaucoma. While the direct causal link between Apraclonidine and glaucoma remains indirect, the cumulative evidence points to a pathway where off-label use of Apraclonidine may contribute to the development of glaucoma through increased ocular hypertension.",
